WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.720 --> 00:00:04.980
Spider-Man: Far from Home has finally arrived,
and it's a lot to take in.

00:00:05.120 --> 00:00:08.660
If you're wondering what those post-credits
scenes mean for Peter Parker and the future

00:00:08.660 --> 00:00:11.000
of the Marvel Cinematic Universe, don't worry.

00:00:11.220 --> 00:00:12.680
We've got the answers you need.

00:00:12.840 --> 00:00:17.020
And if your Peter tingle is… tingling, that's
because there are spoilers ahead!

00:00:17.960 --> 00:00:20.800
Bad luck is a hallmark of Spider-Man stories.

00:00:20.800 --> 00:00:25.019
This is particularly true in Spider-Man: Far
from Home, where bad luck follows him home

00:00:25.020 --> 00:00:26.600
and into the mid-credits scene.

00:00:26.840 --> 00:00:31.619
After the credits roll, his date with MJ is
interrupted by a new bulletin that introduces

00:00:31.620 --> 00:00:35.520
none other than J. Jonah Jameson to the Marvel
Cinematic Universe.

00:00:35.660 --> 00:00:40.840
Good news to fans everywhere: JJJ is played
by J.K. Simmons, reprising his fan-favorite

00:00:40.840 --> 00:00:42.460
role from the Sam Raimi trilogy.

00:00:42.620 --> 00:00:46.640
Jameson has a video that Mysterio doctored
to make it look like Spider-Man was behind

00:00:46.700 --> 00:00:49.340
the London attack, and he has no problem showing
it.

00:00:49.460 --> 00:00:53.850
He also has no qualms about letting the world
know that Spider-Man is Peter Parker, outing

00:00:53.850 --> 00:00:55.260
the teenager to the world.

00:00:55.260 --> 00:00:59.470
Now, in addition to whatever supervillain
he battles next, Peter Parker will have Jonah

00:00:59.470 --> 00:01:04.440
staring him down, trying to ruin his day and
his life at every turn, and that adds a whole

00:01:04.440 --> 00:01:06.850
new level of conflict for future films.

00:01:07.080 --> 00:01:08.860
"Spider-Man: Hero or menace?"

00:01:10.480 --> 00:01:11.020
"Menace?

00:01:11.420 --> 00:01:13.100
He was protecting all those people?"

00:01:13.100 --> 00:01:15.100
"You take the pictures, I'll make up the headlines,
okay?"

00:01:15.800 --> 00:01:20.640
The introduction of J. Jonah Jameson and the
Daily Bugle into Peter Parker's New York City

00:01:20.640 --> 00:01:23.800
is a bold move forward for this little corner
of the MCU.

00:01:23.920 --> 00:01:28.380
In many past Spider-Man films, Peter at least
had the benefit of being Spider-Man anonymously,

00:01:28.380 --> 00:01:31.520
while also managing to work for the Bugle
as a photographer.

00:01:31.640 --> 00:01:35.820
That doesn't seem to be something that would
fly here unless he's really good with a fake

00:01:35.820 --> 00:01:36.320
ID.

00:01:36.320 --> 00:01:40.060
What this suggests is that we're about to
enter an era during which all of New York

00:01:40.060 --> 00:01:42.620
City is automatically suspicious of Spider-Man.

00:01:42.760 --> 00:01:47.400
Worse, it's a world in which Peter will have
to lay low and keep any superheroics in the

00:01:47.400 --> 00:01:48.400
shadows.

00:01:48.400 --> 00:01:52.030
It calls into question every aspect of his
life, from his membership in the Avengers

00:01:52.030 --> 00:01:54.900
to his remaining years in high school, and
his entire future.

00:01:55.120 --> 00:01:58.650
He's going to have to find a way around this,
and perhaps more than any other teaser in

00:01:58.650 --> 00:02:02.380
the film's credits, this condition carries
the most weight moving forward.

00:02:12.280 --> 00:02:16.990
The mid-credits scene gives us a big tease
about the future of Spider-Man, but the post-credits

00:02:16.990 --> 00:02:20.780
scene in Far from Home adds an even bigger
hint about the future of the MCU.

00:02:21.069 --> 00:02:25.760
As Fury and Hill drive away from their London
operation, Hill's face changes to reveal that

00:02:25.760 --> 00:02:27.560
she's actually the skrull, Soren.

00:02:27.780 --> 00:02:32.200
Fury doesn't look shocked at all, and then
we see why: He shifts too, and reveals that

00:02:32.200 --> 00:02:34.200
he's the Skrull known as Talos.

00:02:34.200 --> 00:02:38.200
Audiences last saw these skrulls in Captain
Marvel, where they were last seen searching

00:02:38.200 --> 00:02:39.200
for a new home.

00:02:39.200 --> 00:02:43.069
Now we know that Skrulls are back on Earth
after the events of Captain Marvel, and they're

00:02:43.069 --> 00:02:47.360
here in a capacity that allows them to impersonate
some of the most important people in the world.

00:02:47.630 --> 00:02:51.440
The scene doesn't tell us exactly why they're
here, but it's clear that they've agreed to

00:02:51.460 --> 00:02:55.160
impersonate Hill and Fury as they conduct
some kind of mission for Nick Fury.

00:02:56.040 --> 00:03:00.770
At one point early in Spider-Man: Far from
Home, Fury mentions something about Kree sleeper

00:03:00.770 --> 00:03:01.620
cells to Hill.

00:03:01.770 --> 00:03:05.960
When it's revealed that Fury is actually Talos,
that throwaway line is revealed to have a

00:03:05.960 --> 00:03:07.160
much deeper meaning.

00:03:07.410 --> 00:03:11.800
We have no idea just yet exactly what the
Skrulls are doing back on Earth, but if Fury's

00:03:11.800 --> 00:03:15.920
reference to the Kree is correct, their old
enemies are also hanging around the planet

00:03:15.920 --> 00:03:16.500
as well.

00:03:16.660 --> 00:03:21.480
Captain Marvel established an age-old conflict
between these two alien empires, which the

00:03:21.489 --> 00:03:25.110
Kree won, and made the Skrulls into refugees
looking for a homeland.

00:03:25.110 --> 00:03:29.769
Now, twenty five years later, both civilizations
could be experiencing different circumstances

00:03:29.769 --> 00:03:31.400
that we're not fully aware of yet.

00:03:31.500 --> 00:03:35.760
Talos' mention of hidden Kree cells while
in disguise as Fury suggests that they could

00:03:35.760 --> 00:03:36.840
still be enemies.

00:03:37.020 --> 00:03:41.480
That adds an important context to this reveal
that future films will no doubt build on.

00:03:42.180 --> 00:03:46.740
Far from Home's post-credits scene confirms
for audiences that Talos impersonated Fury

00:03:46.740 --> 00:03:50.840
on Earth while the super-spy lived with the
rest of the Skrulls in space for at least

00:03:50.840 --> 00:03:53.120
the duration of the film, if not for longer.

00:03:53.360 --> 00:03:56.460
What the scene doesn't establish is what happened
to Maria Hill.

00:03:56.460 --> 00:04:00.520
Given her tendency to work in cooperation
with Fury, we can hazard a guess that she's

00:04:00.530 --> 00:04:04.520
somewhere on the same ship, helping with whatever
mission Fury's working on.

00:04:04.520 --> 00:04:09.230
Still, given that the MCU juggles plenty of
plotlines at once, we can't necessarily assume

00:04:09.230 --> 00:04:10.900
the two are still joined at the hip.

00:04:10.900 --> 00:04:13.680
Perhaps she's taking her own time off somewhere
else.

00:04:13.680 --> 00:04:15.660
Whatever the case, her exclusion is noteworthy.

00:04:16.020 --> 00:04:20.410
What's also noteworthy is the film's repeated
insistence that The Avengers just don't seem

00:04:20.410 --> 00:04:21.440
to be a thing right now.

00:04:21.560 --> 00:04:26.180
This is reinforced in the post-credits scene,
in which Talos tells Fury that people kept

00:04:26.180 --> 00:04:29.250
asking him where the Avengers were and he
didn't know what to say.

00:04:29.250 --> 00:04:33.860
Obviously the roster would have changed significantly
in the wake of Endgame, but numerous heroes

00:04:33.860 --> 00:04:35.540
stayed on Earth at the end of that film.

00:04:35.740 --> 00:04:39.460
You'd think that Sam Wilson would have put
a group together as the new Captain America.

00:04:39.460 --> 00:04:41.430
So… where are the Avengers?

00:04:41.430 --> 00:04:45.420
We'll just have to see if the next MCU film
to arrive gives us any more answers.

